    <article class="tale-body">
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="conversation"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>00</span><h2>Yadu asks a wanderer</h2></div><p>In the Bhagavata Purana, Krishna answers Uddhava by recalling an older exchange. King Yadu sees an Avadhuta wandering freely and asks how he came by such wisdom and ease. The wanderer replies that he has learned from many teachers by observing the world closely.</p><p>He names twenty-four in verses 11.7.33–35. Earth, air and sky stand beside animals and people. Their lessons are sometimes examples to follow and sometimes warnings about what binds a person. The teaching is addressed through a renunciant’s way of life; its images invite reflection without turning every ancient ascetic instruction into a rule for all readers.</p><p>The Sanskrit passage calls the teacher an Avadhuta or wandering Brahmin. The supplied Gita Press Hindi explanation identifies him as Dattatreya. This guide keeps that distinction visible rather than adding the name to the verses themselves.</p><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#avadhuta-frame">The conversation and list: 11.7.19–36 →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="first-eight"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>Earth to pigeon</h2></div><p>The first eight teachers are explained in chapter 7. The elements offer images of endurance, openness and clarity. The pigeon’s story turns observation into a warning about attachment that overcomes judgment.</p><ol class="teacher-list" start="1">{% for teacher in avadhutaTeachers %}{% if teacher.chapter == 7 %}<li id="teacher-{{ teacher.id }}"><div class="teacher-heading"><h3>{{ teacher.name }}</h3><small>{{ teacher.verses }}</small></div><p>{{ teacher.lesson }}</p></li>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</ol><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#avadhuta-first-eight">Read chapter 11.7 and its eight teachers →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="middle-nine"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>Python to Pingala</h2></div><p>Chapter 8 moves among living creatures and desire. It closes with Pingala’s extended account: she watches and waits through an anxious night, then finds rest by releasing the hope that has held her.</p><ol class="teacher-list" start="9">{% for teacher in avadhutaTeachers %}{% if teacher.chapter == 8 %}<li id="teacher-{{ teacher.id }}"><div class="teacher-heading"><h3>{{ teacher.name }}</h3><small>{{ teacher.verses }}</small></div><p>{{ teacher.lesson }}</p></li>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</ol><p class="reader-note">The examples of the elephant and deer are voiced from an ascetic setting concerned with sensory restraint. Pingala is a person in the narrative, not merely a symbol: the source gives her a long speech and the decision to change her outlook.</p><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#avadhuta-middle-nine">Read chapter 11.8 and its nine teachers →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="final-seven"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>Kurara to wasp</h2></div><p>The final seven, in chapter 9, bring the guide closer to everyday life. A bird relinquishes its prize, a young woman quiets her bangles, and an artisan’s attention stays with his craft even as a king passes nearby.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/avadhuta-arrow-maker.webp" alt="An imagined arrow maker absorbed in shaping a shaft while a small procession passes in the background" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE ARROW MAKER’S ATTENTION · AI-GENERATED NARRATIVE INTERPRETATION, NOT A HISTORICAL PORTRAIT</figcaption></figure><ol class="teacher-list" start="18">{% for teacher in avadhutaTeachers %}{% if teacher.chapter == 9 %}<li id="teacher-{{ teacher.id }}"><div class="teacher-heading"><h3>{{ teacher.name }}</h3><small>{{ teacher.verses }}</small></div><p>{{ teacher.lesson }}</p></li>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</ol><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#avadhuta-final-seven">Read chapter 11.9 and its seven teachers →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-chapter" id="body-teacher"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>The body also teaches</h2></div><p>After completing the twenty-four, the Avadhuta turns to his own body as another occasion for discernment. In 11.9.24–25 he calls it a teacher because its changing, vulnerable condition reminds him not to cling to it, even while it makes inquiry possible.</p><p>The body is <strong>outside the numbered list of twenty-four</strong>. Keeping it here preserves the source’s count while allowing the closing thought to be heard.</p><a class="cosmology-source" href="/sources.html#avadhuta-body">The closing reflection: 11.9.24–25 →</a></section>
      <section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>Attention can turn<br><em>the world into a classroom.</em></h2><p>Which ordinary thing has changed the way you act or think because you looked at it closely? This question is an editorial invitation, separate from the text’s specific instructions to an Avadhuta.</p></section>
      <section class="tale-reflection" id="reading-note"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>Twenty-four examples,<br><em>one textual setting.</em></h2><p>The summaries follow only the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi Bhagavata Purana, Volume 2, chapters 11.7–9. They condense the passage in original English prose. The teaching’s ascetic voice and devotional context matter; these lessons are not presented as natural science or universal rules for family life.</p><p>The wasp and spider are spiritual analogies in the text. The illustrations are imagined, and the closing reflection is editorial. <a href="/sources.html#avadhuta-teachers">Edition details and verse locations</a> are collected in the source library.</p></section>
